ow that brand has  seen this in his visions he's seen the  Mad King in his visions and we know that  Jamie has been haunted by this idea of  being known as the Kingslayer before  that happens I expect that Brienne will  make a passionate plea to everyone and  that Jaime will be moved by this  obviously there is love between Jaime  and Brienne but I think this moment will  make that clear for everyone to see then  when Bronn arrives he'll tell Jaime that  Cersei sent him there to kill him and  that will finally be when Jaime's love  for Cersei truly comes to an end since I  think he will die and I think it's  likely to be in brianne's  arms I think this could be bronze real  importance for the end of the story in  my thinking it has to be clear to Jaime  in his heart  and in his head who the woman he loves  is I think the crossbow could probably  play a part in this as well  of course they used that as a call back  in an episode with a crazy amount of  call back but you have to admit that it  seemed like a weird touch to include my  guess is that this is what bran would  use as proof of Circe's intention Jamie  left Cersei to go north  but he's loved her for as long as he can  remember more or less when he told bran  he would die in the arms of the woman he  loved he was obviously talking about  Cersei I think when he sees the crossbow  he'll have to confront the truth and  this will push him in brianne's  direction now admittedly I have been a  Jamie as the bound car fan for a long  time I've always thought it was fitting  in for both characters and yes I  understand they changed what Maggie said  in the show I actually made a video  about that a few years ago but I still  think that it's baked into Circe's  motivations what I mean is that I don't  think you can create an adaptation of  the character and the books without  having this be part of her motivation  whether it's explicitly stated or not  Cersei is a paranoid human being it's  what drives her and this idea of him  killing her has made it difficult to  decide when Jamie might actually go out  but as far as dying in brianne's arms is  concerned I guess it doesn't matter he  could die in the battle fighting by her  side or you could die later in King's  Landing but either way I think Brienne  will be involved with his end I also  have always liked the idea that Brienne  would fill out his pages in the white  book but who knows that the Kingsguard  or the white book will even exist when  all is said and done the important part  is that bran will explain what the Queen  is up to to Jaime and he will realize  that Brienne is actually the woman that  he loved I find this much more  satisfying than a bran character that is  facing a dilemma of killing his friends  or not but what do you think do you  think this was just included because we  have to have a good ending for bran he  has to decide to do the right thing or  not depending on which side you think  he'll end up on or do you think there  might be something to this idea that  he's really just a device to get Jaime  straightened up on who he's in love with  before he actually  bites the bullet let me know in the  comments I thought it was interesting  during the red carpet that a lot of the  media outlets were reporting about what  he said about his character he was  saying that the fans might not like him  so much anymore you know I'm  paraphrasing obviously and that the  filming wasn't that difficult for him  after seeing episode 1 it became clear  that he was saying that as it related to  the first episode not his entire  character arc for the season this  actually adds a little bit more fuel to  that fire because we're at the time I  thought maybe he was gonna stay in  King's Landing and maybe even be the one  who shoots down a dragon with the  scorpions he may have just been trying  to pull the wool over our eyes a little  bit in those interviews knowing that he  was gonna go north and hook up with  Jamie that's 